{"id":"2dfdbd99-3aff-45f3-ae19-931541f9064a","object":"chat.completion","created":1785005900,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"```json\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n\\n    # Build lookup and children\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n['parent']\\n        if p is None:\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n        else:\\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\\n\\n    if root_id is None:\\n        raise ValueError('No root node')\\n\\n    # Sort by depth descending, then by id string for tie-break\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n\\n    removed_ids = []\\n    removed_set = set()\\n\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        # Root or not removable → skip\\n        if nid == root_id or not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Check if all children are already removed (or none)\\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\\n        if any(cid not in removed_set for cid in child_ids):\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Check if after removal each required symbol still appears somewhere\\n        symbol = n['symbol']\\n        # Count occurrences of each required symbol in currently present nodes\\n        # (present = not in removed_set, including this node)\\n        present_symbols = {}\\n        for node in node_map.values():\\n            if node['id'] not in removed_set and node['id'] != nid:\\n                present_symbols[node['symbol']] = present_symbols.get(node['symbol'], 0) + 1\\n        # Add this node if we are not removing it yet (we are checking)\\n        present_symbols[symbol] = present_symbols.get(symbol, 0) + 1\\n\\n        feasible = True\\n        for req_sym in required_symbols:\\n            count = present_symbols.get(req_sym, 0)\\n            if count == 0:\\n                feasible = False\\n                break\\n            # If this node carries the required symbol, after removal count becomes count-1\\n            if req_sym == symbol and count == 1:\\n                feasible = False\\n                break\\n\\n        if not feasible:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Remove the node\\n        removed_ids.append(nid)\\n        removed_set.add(nid)\\n\\n    # All nodes not removed are retained, sorted by id\\n    retained_ids = sorted(\\n        n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed_set\\n    )\\n\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_ids,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_ids\\n    }\\n\"\n}\n```","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for SE-PE-01. The task is grammar-guided node reduction on a tree. Input: case object with nodes list (each node has id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility) and required_symbols list. Output: {removed_node_ids: list of strings, retained_node_ids: list of strings}. We need to simulate a reduction algorithm: try to remove removable nodes (and possibly their subtrees) while preserving that all required symbols remain represented (i.e., at least one node with that symbol remains). Also must not remove root, not remove nodes with retained children (i.e., children that are kept). The order: deeper nodes before shallower nodes (i.e., post-order). Use node ID as tiebreaker (stable). \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we consider each node in order (depth descending, ties by ID lexicographically? The spec says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So for nodes at same depth, sort by ID string. Then for each candidate node that is removable (and not root? root not removable anyway), we consider removing it (i.e., deleting it and its subtree?) Actually \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" but from description, it seems we are removing nodes entirely. But we need to ensure that required symbols remain after removal. The \"interestingness oracle\" is represented by required_symbols: we must keep at least one node of each required symbol. So if removing a node that is the only node with a required symbol, we cannot remove it. Also cannot remove root. Also \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So if a node has children that we have decided to retain (i.e., not removed), then we cannot remove that node because removing it would also remove its children? But we could also remove the children first. The algorithm: traverse nodes in post-order (deeper first), considering each removable node, check if its removal would cause any required symbol to be missing from the remaining nodes. Also must not remove if any of its children are retained. But note: if we process deeper nodes first, when we consider a parent, its children might already have been removed. So \"retained children\" means children that are still present (not yet removed). So we need to simulate the reduction step by step. Since we are only removing nodes (not replacing), the algorithm is: sort all nodes by depth descending, then by ID. For each node in that order, if it is removable and not root, and all required symbols are still present after removing it (i.e., check if there are other nodes with each required symbol), and it has no retained children (i.e., all its children are already removed or none), then we remove it (add to removed list). Otherwise we retain it. At the end, retained_node_ids are all node IDs not removed. We need to return sorted retained IDs? The spec: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids sorted, removed_node_ids in order of removal (i.e., the order we removed them). So we iterate in the given order, when we decide to remove, we append to removed list. At end, retained are all that are not removed, sorted.\n\nNeed to verify with the provided fixture examples. They have small trees, but we can't see expected outputs. Just implement.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Parse case to get nodes list and required_symbols.\n2. Build a dict from id to node for quick access.\n3. Build parent-child relationships: for each node, children list.\n4. Determine root: node with parent null.\n5. Validate: root exists, etc. Raise ValueError if invalid? The spec says \"raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case\". But we can just proceed.\n6. Create set of node IDs that are still present (initially all). We'll maintain a set of removed IDs.\n7. Sort all nodes by depth descending, then by id (string compare). Use sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id'])).\n8. Iterate over that list:\n   - If node id in removed set: skip (already removed as subtree of parent removal? But we are removing only individually, so we won't remove children if parent is removed. But we might have removed a parent later? No, because we process deeper first, so children are processed before parents. If we remove a child, it's fine. If we don't remove a child, then parent may be considered later. But if we remove a child, parent still there. However, if we remove a parent, we must also remove its children? The spec says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if we remove a parent, we are essentially removing that node and its entire subtree? The algorithm might remove a node entirely, meaning its children are also removed. But the spec says we cannot remove a node that has retained children. So we only remove a node if all its children are already removed (or it has no children). So we must track whether children are retained (i.e., not removed yet). In our iteration, when we consider a node, we can check if any of its children are still present (not in removed set). If yes, skip (do not remove). Otherwise, we can tentatively remove it, but we must also check required symbols after removal (including removal of its subtree? But if we remove the node, its children are already removed? Actually if children are not removed yet, we cannot remove parent. So children must already be removed for parent to be removable. So removing parent just removes that node, children already gone. So required symbol check: we need to see if after removing this node, there remains another node with the same symbol (if it's the only one, we cannot remove). But also if required symbols are present in children that are already removed, they are gone. So we need to check the current set of present nodes (excluding this node and any of its descendants that are already removed? But if children are already removed, they are not present. So we just check if any other present node (other than this node) has the same symbol for each required symbol. Also need to ensure that after removal, each required symbol is still present somewhere (maybe in other branches). So implement: For candidate node, if any of its children (by building children list at start) are still present (i.e., id not in removed set), skip. Then check: for each required symbol, count the number of nodes currently present (including candidate) that have that symbol. Subtract one if candidate symbol equals that required symbol. If any required symbol would become 0, skip. Otherwise, remove it: add id to removed set, append to removed list.\n\n9. After processing all nodes, retained_node_ids = sorted([n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed_set]).\n10. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_list, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}.\n\nNote: The root is not removable (removable false) so it won't be removed. Good.\n\nAlso need to handle case where required_symbols may include symbols that are only in the root? Root symbol is 'root'. required_symbols includes 'root' and 's1'. So root is always required, can't be removed anyway.\n\nPotential edge: Multiple nodes with same symbol; we need at least one.\n\nNow we must output Python source code for solve(case). The function should import nothing extra; use only standard lib.\n\nWe will write the code as a string and embed it in JSON under \"implementation\".\n\nMake sure to escape properly.\n\nLet's produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":2271,"total_tokens":4440,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1616},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}